{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And you shall see.</b> It should have said 'and you shall choose' (ואתה תבחר), as it says in the narrative, ״ויבחר משה אנשי חיל״ — and Moses chose men of valor. But because man sees with the eyes and HaShem sees the heart, therefore it needed to use the language of תחזה, which is a term of prophecy, from the language of מחזה and חזון (vision); and the intention is that prophecy would be required for this selection. Or one may say: ואתה תחזה — by recognition of faces, through the wisdom of physiognomy (חכמת הפרצוף), which Moses knew and was expert in, since he was the most perfect of the perfect, the greatest of the prophets, and the sage of secrets; therefore He warned him with the word ואתה — meaning: you yourself, and not by way of command to another." "<b>From all the people, men of valor.</b> All the qualities that he goes on to mention — יראי אלהים (God-fearers), אנשי אמת (men of truth), שונאי בצע (haters of unjust gain) — are all included within אנשי חיל (men of valor). For אנשי חיל is what those who know how to lead the people in judgment are called, for it is impossible for judgment to be sound without these qualities: that they be God-fearers. And as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: a judge must see himself as though a sword lies on his neck from above and Gehenna is open to him from below, and he stands in the middle — if he merits, he is delivered from both; if he does not merit, he is handed over to both. They must also be אנשי אמת — men of truth, that is, lovers of truth who choose it, who will agree only to it and will not accept a false claim, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 23:7): ״מדבר שקר תרחק״ — 'from a false matter you shall distance yourself' — which is a warning to the judge. And they must also be שונאי בצע — haters of בצע, that is, haters of money; and it need not mean others' money but even their own money. In the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said: even if he burns my stacks, even if he cuts down my saplings — I judge him fairly. According to this, the word בצע would be a noun meaning money, as in (Genesis 37:26): ״מה בצע כי נהרוג את אחינו״ — whose Aramaic translation is 'what profit (ממון) do we gain?' And likewise (Micah 4:13): ״והחרמתי לה׳ בצעם״ — 'and I will devote their money (ממונם) to YHWH.' Or the word בצע could be a noun meaning robbery, that is, that they should be haters of robbery and oppression, and not tolerate oppression and violence in their minds. Come and see how great is the power and the magnitude of the excellence of character traits: the righteous and the prophets were not praised in the Torah — such as Noah, Abraham, Jacob, and others — with attributes of wisdom, understanding, and knowledge, but rather with attributes of character traits. Of Noah it is said (Genesis 6:9): ״איש צדיק תמים היה״ — 'a righteous man, perfect was he'; of Abraham it is said (Genesis 17:1): ״והיה תמים״ — 'and be perfect'; of Jacob (Genesis 25:27): ״איש תם״ — 'a wholehearted man'; and likewise of Moses (Numbers 12:3): ״ענו מאד״ — 'exceedingly humble.' And all this is to teach that the essence of wisdom is uprightness of character traits, just as the essence of the tree is the fruit. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded on (Psalms 111:10): ״שכר טוב לכל עושיהם״ — 'a good reward to all who do them' — it was not said לכל לומדיהם ('to all who learn them') but rather לכל עושיהם ('to all who do them')."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:58.861558616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}